# GUI table implementation TODO

Status: Phases 1 through 6 complete (owning table, divider strategies, composer/render lifecycle, opt-in input/nested focus, and controls-only HUD adoption). P7 acceptance remains pending, including actual GPU display and in-game visual/input checks.

Design source: [GUI table proposal](GuiElementTable.proposal.md).

## Delivery scope

First deliver the reusable owning table, divider-rendering strategies, automated coverage, and migration of the build-mode controls content.

Keep snapping/rotation migration as a separate follow-up after the first delivery passes its verification gates. Room information is a future consumer, not part of this implementation.

Approved constraints:

- Derive `GuiElementTable` directly from `GuiElement`.
- Accept collections of elements grouped by row; infer columns without requiring placeholders.
- Own accepted children and manage their composition, rendering, input, focus, and disposal.
- Register only the table with the outer composer.
- Keep table code independent of the HUD and collapsible sections.
- Delegate all style-specific divider drawing through `ITableDividerRenderer`.
- Preserve existing HUD appearance and input behavior during the initial migration.

## Working locations

- Component and supporting types: [UI source](../VanillaBuildingExpanded/src/UI/).
- Component tests: [UI tests](../VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests/UI/).
- Initial integration: [BuildModeHud.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded/src/BuildBrush/Hud/BuildModeHud.cs).
- Existing section contract: [CollapsibleSection.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded/src/UI/CollapsibleSection.cs).
- Existing regressions: [section tests](../VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests/UI/CollapsibleSectionTests.cs) and [collapse-button tests](../VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests/UI/GuiElementCollapseButtonTests.cs).

Supporting type and test-file names below are tentative. Keep related small types together where appropriate; do not add a generic container framework or test-only production abstractions.

## Dependencies

| Phase | Prerequisites |
| --- | --- |
| P1: Finalizing contracts | None |
| P2: Implementing layout and ownership | P1 |
| P3: Implementing divider strategies | P1, P2 layout geometry |
| P4: Implementing composition and rendering | P2, P3 |
| P5: Implementing input and focus | P1, P2 |
| P6: Migrating controls content | P4, P5 |
| P7: Verifying the first delivery | P3, P4, P5, P6 |
| F1: Migrating snapping and rotation | P7 |

Write focused tests with each phase rather than deferring all coverage to P7. Rendering and input work may proceed independently once layout and ownership contracts are established.

## P1 - Finalizing contracts

- [x] Define the smallest public/internal surface for row addition, preferred measurements, column configuration, measured size, arrangement, and table-local lookup.
- [x] Support element-array and enumerable row overloads, plus construction from a collection of rows, without ambiguous overloads.
- [x] Specify optional cell descriptors for explicit preferred sizes and child keys.
- [x] Resolve the remaining input-policy API before writing event routing: ordinary rows must be convenient for passive information, while callers must be able to explicitly opt controls into input. Do not infer clickability solely from `Focusable` or concrete element types.
- [x] Specify how eligible focusable children join nested tab traversal and how passive children are excluded.
- [x] Define ownership transfer precisely for successful row acceptance, failed additions, constructor failure, abandoned tables, and failed composer registration.
- [x] Define how preferred-size changes and renderer replacement request safe deferred recomposition without forcing a full HUD rebuild for value-only changes.
- [x] Confirm cancellation/focus-loss mechanisms available on arbitrary native child elements; document any required child capability rather than assuming every element exposes `CancelPress()`.
- [x] Record finalized contracts in the proposal before implementation depends on them; seek approval for any material behavior change.

Exit gate: API and ownership rules are unambiguous, including passive/interactive behavior and failure paths.

Completion evidence: see the proposal's "Phase 1 contracts" section for finalized signatures, ownership/failure rules, deferred invalidation, and native-source references. The user selected passive defaults with explicit input flags and required non-activating pointer-cancellation callbacks. Complete-row construction validates all inputs before ownership transfer. `TryAddTable` distinguishes conditional skips from registration and documents native partial-registration failures. No production code or automated tests were added in this phase.

## P2 - Implementing layout and ownership

- [x] Add the sealed `GuiElementTable : GuiElement` and minimal row/cell/column data types.
- [x] Snapshot supplied row collections and validate each entire row before accepting ownership.
- [x] Reject empty rows, null elements, duplicate instances or keys, conflicting parentage, and self-referencing bounds.
- [x] Validate finite, nonnegative sizes and gaps; reject unsupported percentage sizing, wrapping-dependent measurement, and automatic text height.
- [x] Preserve preferred sizes separately from arranged child coordinates.
- [x] Infer column count from the longest row and preserve row/element order.
- [x] Implement auto widths with optional minimums and fixed widths with explicit overflow errors.
- [x] Calculate row heights from tallest preferred content and minimum row height.
- [x] Arrange top-left-aligned cells using logical units, with no trailing gaps, implicit spanning, or phantom cells.
- [x] Ensure empty tables measure zero by zero and repeated layout is stable.
- [x] Expose table-local child lookup without exposing a mutable flat child collection.
- [x] Freeze structural mutation after composer registration for the first version.
- [x] Implement idempotent ownership cleanup for accepted rows and unregistered tables; ensure complete-row constructor validation failure transfers no children and leaks no resources.
- [x] Add dimension-only and instrumented-child tests for layout, validation, lookup, atomic row acceptance, and disposal.

Required exact layout fixture:

```text
Preferred cell sizes:
Row 1: (40 x 12), (100 x 16)
Row 2: (90 x 30)

Auto-column minimums: 88, 200
Column gap: 12
Row gap: 4
Minimum row height: 20

Expected columns: 90, 200
Expected rows: 20, 30
Expected table size: 302 x 54
Expected cell origins:
Row 1: (0, 0), (102, 0)
Row 2: (0, 24)
```

Exit gate: exact dimensions, short-row behavior, failure ownership, and repeatability pass without a live GPU.

Completion evidence:

- Implementation: [GuiElementTable.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded/src/UI/GuiElementTable.cs) and [TableLayout.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded/src/UI/TableLayout.cs).
- Tests: [GuiElementTableTests.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests/UI/GuiElementTableTests.cs), including the exact fixture above, complete-constructor enumeration/validation failures, non-finite totals, and cleanup failures.
- The focused UI test run passed 61 tests with zero failures or skips, including existing section and collapse-button regressions. It also built the production and test projects; unrelated existing compiler warnings remain.
- The auto-height rejection test constructs a native text/font object, requiring the installed game's Cairo library on the test process's DLL path. It uses no live GPU.
- The internal registration marker and row freeze are implemented and tested. The actual composer registration helper remains P4 work; do not register this partial component directly.
- No textures, divider drawing, input routing, or HUD migration were implemented in P2.

Validation command, run from the repository root using the existing `VINTAGE_STORY` environment setting:

```powershell
$env:PATH = "$env:VINTAGE_STORY\Lib;$env:PATH"
dotnet test .\VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests\VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests.csproj --no-restore --filter "FullyQualifiedName~VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests.UI" --verbosity minimal
```

## P3 - Implementing divider geometry and strategies

- [x] Add `ITableDividerRenderer` and a read-only divider-geometry snapshot.
- [x] Generate each row boundary once across table width, at the midpoint of its row gap.
- [x] Generate cell boundaries only between adjacent populated cells in each row, spanning that row's allocated height.
- [x] Handle zero gaps, empty tables, single rows, and single-cell rows without phantom segments.
- [x] Convert segment coordinates to table-local device units once and expose GUI scale for logical stroke/dash sizing.
- [x] Implement the no-op default renderer.
- [x] Implement solid and dashed renderers as separate strategies to exercise the extension point; keep their artwork configurable rather than treating it as approved HUD styling.
- [x] Put boundary-kind selection, colors, widths, dashes, pixel alignment, and intersection handling in renderer implementations or immutable options, not the table.
- [x] Validate renderer settings explicitly; allow sharing immutable resource-free renderer instances.
- [x] Add recording-renderer geometry tests and focused Cairo tests where the existing environment supports them.
- [x] Verify that injecting an additional test renderer requires no concrete-style checks or style switches in the table.

For the P2 fixture, expect one horizontal boundary at `y = 22` across width 302 and one vertical boundary at `x = 96` spanning the first row's height of 20, before GUI scaling. The shorter second row contributes no vertical divider.

Exit gate: styles are independently replaceable, geometry is exact, and styles do not affect layout.

Completion evidence:

- Added [TableDividerGeometry.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded/src/UI/TableDividerGeometry.cs) and [TableDividerRenderers.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded/src/UI/TableDividerRenderers.cs), including XML API documentation.
- The table exposes `GetDividerGeometry(guiScale)` for settled-layout snapshots, a non-null `DividerRenderer`, and artwork invalidation. Its internal `DrawDividers` entry point delegates through the interface with clipping and drawing-state/path restoration; P4 will connect it to child composition and texture upload.
- No-op, solid, and dashed strategies use immutable settings and retain no native drawing resources. Boundary selection and line mechanics remain outside the table.
- [TableDividerTests.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests/UI/TableDividerTests.cs) covers exact geometry at scales 1, 1.25, 1.5, and 2; collection snapshots; renderer injection; clipping; state/path restoration after errors; dash phase; single-blend intersections; and invalid settings.
- A 100-row fractional-track regression exposed differing accumulation orders in measurement and placement. Both now use the same computed track origins/extents, also used by divider geometry; no tolerance or clipping fallback hides inconsistent layout.
- The focused UI run passed 110 tests with zero failures or skips and built production/test projects. Offscreen Cairo pixels were verified using the installed native library and the P2 validation command. Unrelated existing compiler warnings remain.
- No composer registration helper, child texture composition, dynamic child forwarding, or HUD changes were added in P3.

## P4 - Implementing composition and rendering

- [x] Add composer integration that registers only the table as an interactive element, including when its content is passive.
- [x] Complete measurement and arrangement before composer world-bound calculation and surface allocation.
- [x] Mark table bounds as a drawing surface and compose children in the correct local coordinate system.
- [x] Allocate/update the table-owned static texture; skip allocation when either drawable dimension is zero.
- [x] Draw dividers through the interface before static child artwork, with clipping and Cairo save/restore around the call.
- [x] Compose every child in drawable tables and forward dynamic rendering in stable draw order without registering children with the composer.
- [x] Match native alpha conventions and propagate clip-bound changes to existing children.
- [x] Implement deferred artwork invalidation and texture reuse/release through the normal lifecycle.
- [x] Verify renderer replacement rebuilds artwork only, while child value updates fitting reserved bounds do not trigger table reflow.
- [x] Guard repeated disposal and post-disposal callbacks; release texture and children once even when the composer disposes the table through multiple collections.
- [x] Add lifecycle tests using counters/mocks and offscreen drawing where practical; reserve actual GPU upload/render verification for the in-game pass.

Exit gate passed in automated coverage: real native static/dynamic text composes and renders through the table, preparation/composition hooks are not duplicated within a native pass, and scale/clipping are verified with Cairo pixels and render-API mocks. Actual GPU display remains an explicitly pending P7 check.

P4 evidence and implementation notes:

- [GuiElementTableComposerExtensions.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded/src/UI/GuiElementTableComposerExtensions.cs) implements explicit-result registration, preflight checks, conditional skips, and the partial-native-insertion ownership contract. Registration performs no texture allocation.
- The table owns a lazy reusable texture, composes dividers before static child artwork, forwards dynamic and post-render stages in stable order, and restores intersecting table/enclosing scissors on failure.
- Native preparation cannot change reserved logical footprints. A fit-to-children composer regression verifies correct dimensions before parent surface allocation. Zero drawable extent releases stale artwork and skips texture creation and child composition/rendering.
- Static rebuild requests coalesce, including renderer replacement. A native dynamic-text update uploads only its own texture and does not reflow or recompose the table. Invalidations raised during child composition remain pending.
- Premultiplied and straight-alpha pixels are tested. The installed Cairo wrapper's DemulAlpha helper uses the wrong alpha byte on little-endian Windows; a table-local native-endian conversion avoids that bug, preserves transparency and RGB channels, and leaves native libraries unchanged.
- [TableCompositionTests.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests/UI/TableCompositionTests.cs) adds 38 lifecycle/registration cases. The combined UI command recorded in P2 passes 148 tests with zero failures or skips and builds production/test projects. Existing unrelated compiler warnings remain.
- Input routing/focus and HUD migration remain out of scope for P4. The table suppresses the inherited consuming mouse-down fallback, but does not yet route child input.

## P5 - Implementing input and focus

- [x] Route events only to input-eligible children, respecting clipping, handled flags, and visual ordering.
- [x] Preserve pass-through over empty cells, table gaps, and passive children; avoid native base-handler fallbacks that consume those events.
- [x] Track active pointer interaction so release/cancellation reaches its child outside the original bounds.
- [x] Propagate hovered-child cursors and clear stale cursor state.
- [x] Keep tables with no eligible focusable children non-focusable.
- [x] Expose a focusable group when appropriate and implement nested focus, stable tab order, forward/reverse traversal, and exit to the outer composer.
- [x] Propagate focus loss and cancel active interaction using the capability defined in P1.
- [x] Leave the host HUD/dialog in control of whether it routes input to the table.
- [x] Add tests for passive text, non-focusable clickable controls, handled events, clipping, outside release, focus loss, cursor reset, and traversal boundaries.

Exit gate passed in automated coverage: passive content remains input-transparent, opted-in controls receive capture-safe routing, and the concrete native composer correctly enters, traverses, and exits nested focus in both directions.

P5 evidence and implementation notes:

- [GuiElementTable.Input.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded/src/UI/GuiElementTable.Input.cs) keeps substantial input/focus lifecycle code separate from layout/composition while remaining part of the same owning component. Pointer hit order is reverse visual order; keyboard traversal remains row-major.
- Track all down recipients by mouse button, even when they do not mark handled. Release outside bounds is forwarded once, then safely cancelled. Handled releases only cancel. Captured movement is deduplicated and precedes hover-only recipients; outgoing hover receives an exit notification.
- CancelInput clears group/nested focus, cursor, pending selection, and capture before callbacks. The host must invoke it when hiding or disabling input routing or returning the pointer to gameplay. Cleanup is non-activating, reentrancy-guarded, and reports aggregated failures without skipping other recipients.
- Group focusability is available before registration and requires Keyboard eligibility plus native Focusable. Pointer selection survives native group focus assignment; clicking pointer-only content does not select an unrelated keyboard child. Internal focus changes safely cancel outgoing pointer capture.
- Native composer forward/reverse entry, internal Tab handling, boundary exit, and single-outer-group focus preservation are verified. No base mouse-down fallback consumes passive table content.
- Cursor propagation also runs after child rendering, verified through native GuiComposer.Render without mouse movement. Clip changes, pointer exit, cancellation, and disposal clear stale cursor state.
- [TableInputTests.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests/UI/TableInputTests.cs) adds 41 cases; [TableCompositionTests.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests/UI/TableCompositionTests.cs) adds one native-composer cursor case. The combined UI suite passes 190 tests with zero failures or skips and builds production/test projects. Existing unrelated compiler warnings remain.
- No HUD, collapse-section, or gameplay-input routing changes were made. Controls-content migration remains P6; actual in-game verification remains P7.

## P6 - Migrating controls content

- [x] Build one row per keybinding hint with an action label and custom keybinding-drawing element.
- [x] Keep keycap drawing and preferred keybinding measurement in the HUD.
- [x] Use auto-column minimums 88 and 200, column gap 12, minimum row height 20, row gap zero, and the no-op divider renderer.
- [x] Replace controls-column maxima, manual row positioning, and separate row-count height arithmetic with table measurements.
- [x] Wire the table through existing section content callbacks in the HUD; add no table-specific behavior to `CollapsibleSection`.
- [x] Preserve expanded-width measurement while collapsed without leaking an unregistered table or disposing children still owned by a live table.
- [x] Handle table creation, composer clearing, keybinding refresh, GUI-scale changes, close/reopen, and HUD disposal with explicit ownership.
- [x] Preserve pointer routing exclusively to collapse buttons; do not broaden HUD keyboard or mouse handling.
- [x] Add focused HUD/content tests for row construction and sizing where practical, and retain existing section/button regression coverage.

Exit gate passed in automated coverage: controls use the table with the previous dimensions and native label pixels, unchanged keycap drawing, and no intended gameplay-input change. Snapping/rotation remain untouched. Actual in-game appearance and interaction acceptance are still pending in P7.

P6 evidence and implementation notes:

- [BuildModeHud.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded/src/BuildBrush/Hud/BuildModeHud.cs) builds passive custom-drawing row children and delegates all column/row sizing and placement to the table. Section width and height callbacks both consume table measurements.
- Label fonts are scoped to drawing, with no retained child font resources. Native text drawing uses the table-allocated shared label-column width derived from the binding child's arranged origin; this avoids new per-cell line breaks while preserving prior native origin rounding and label behavior. Keycap measurement and drawing remain in the HUD.
- Each ComposeHud build creates one measurement table. Expanded content registers only that table; collapsed content measures expanded width and disposes the unregistered table. Successful tables remain composer-owned; failed builds dispose the local composer and any unregistered table, clear the pending reference, and propagate errors.
- Existing collapse/expand, rebinding, GUI-scale, close/reopen, and shutdown paths are tested. Controls cells remain passive; only section collapse buttons receive free-cursor pointer routing, and HUD keyboard/move/wheel policy is unchanged.
- [BuildModeHudControlsTests.cs](../VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests/UI/BuildModeHudControlsTests.cs) adds 20 cases, including legacy dimension comparisons and native label pixel equivalence at scales 1, 1.25, 1.33, 1.5, and 2, plus concrete native HUD lifecycle and ownership tests. GPU APIs/icon bitmap are mocked; Cairo rasterization and native GUI composition are real.
- Validation command: set process PATH to include the installed game's Lib directory, then run dotnet test against VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests.csproj with --no-restore and filter "FullyQualifiedName~VanillaBuildingExpanded.Tests.UI|FullyQualifiedName~BuildModeKeybindsTests". It passes 216 tests with zero failures or skips and builds production/test projects. Existing unrelated compiler warnings remain.
- No changes to table infrastructure, CollapsibleSection, snapping/rotation content, or gameplay input routing were needed. P7 manual checks remain unchecked.

## P7 - Verifying and closing the first delivery

- [ ] Build the affected production project using the repository's existing .NET 10 setup and game references.
- [ ] Run new table layout, ownership, divider, rendering-lifecycle, and input tests together with existing UI section/button tests and keybinding tests.
- [ ] Use existing xUnit/VSTest tooling; confirm `VINTAGE_STORY` resolves the required installed game assemblies. Do not introduce a new test runner or renderer dependency.
- [ ] Expand test scope only if focused failures or shared lifecycle changes justify it.
- [ ] In game, compare controls appearance and panel dimensions before/after at identical settings.
- [ ] Exercise fractional GUI scales, long labels, alternate key combinations, changed bindings, and repeated collapse/expand and close/reopen cycles.
- [ ] Verify dynamic child values, clipping, no-op dividers, solid/dashed styles, and runtime style replacement in a suitable test host without changing the production HUD's no-op default.
- [ ] Verify empty and partially populated tables, varying row heights, nested focus, and release outside the table.
- [ ] Confirm panel non-button areas still pass input to gameplay and collapse buttons retain their existing behavior.
- [ ] Check for duplicate disposal, stale child references, retained textures, and runtime exceptions during rebuilding and shutdown.
- [ ] Update the proposal with finalized API details and verified implementation status; mark TODO items complete only with supporting evidence.
- [ ] Record automated results separately from in-game checks. Leave unavailable manual checks explicitly pending rather than declaring them passed.

First-delivery completion gate: the owning table and divider extension point are implemented and tested, controls are migrated, and required in-game behavior is verified.

## F1 - Follow-up: migrating snapping and rotation

- [ ] After P7, replace the remaining label/value coordinates with another table.
- [ ] Move snapping/rotation lookups to table-local keys or typed non-owning references.
- [ ] Preserve title handling, unavailable-state colors, dynamic updates, and reserved cell dimensions.
- [ ] Keep the title, icon, panel background, and section chrome outside the table.
- [ ] Repeat targeted regression and visual/input checks for this separate migration.

Do not add room information, wrapping, spanning, outer borders, scrolling, selection, or other deferred features as part of either migration.
